2 until  1991 ukraine was a member of the soviet  union but in 1991 it declared its  independence and became its own  sovereign state in 2004 the orange  revolution led to annulling a  presidential election which was claimed  to be marred by massive corruption voter  intimidation and electoral fraud new  elections were held in 2005 that  eventually led to viktor dushenko  beating the russian-backed victor  yanukovich but janukovic eventually  succeeded in the 2010 election but in  2013 the ukrainian people launched a  protest movement against the corrupt  pro-russian president viktor yanukovych  who was seen as being backed by russian  billionaires who were hostile to nato  not to make this about the united states  but coincidentally during this era  donald trump's campaign manager paul  manafort spent 10 years as one of  yanukovich's advisors of course in 2020  president trump was impeached by the  house after he put on hold 391 million  dollars in congressionally approved  military aid to ukraine but in 2013  ukraine's government was about to sign  an agreement establishing closer ties  with europe but yanukovych backed out  one week before the agreement was to be  signed instead he announced that ukraine  would build a stronger relationship with  russia this announcement reneged on a  campaign promise and yanukovich's  popularity plummeted and after the  euromaidan protest yanakovic fled to  russia and ukraine elected a pro-western  government and in 2014 russia tried to  hack ukraine's central election  commission so that it would present a  far-right candidate who in fact got less  than one percent of the vote as the  winner as historian tim snyder  documented the hack failed but russian  media had been prepared for its success  and russian television went on air with  falsified documents even digital images  that matched what the hack was supposed  to produce when the election gambit  failed russia invaded ukraine and  annexed the territory of crimea and in  2019 former comedian vlodimir zielinski  who on tv played a history teacher who  ran on an anti-corruption platform and  eventually became the president of  ukraine  ran on an anti-corruption platform and  became the president of ukraine and of  course prior to this most recent  invasion zielinski was famous in the  united states for having what everyone  called a perfect phone call with former  president donald trump just an  absolutely perfect phone call then in  february of 2022 russian president  vladimir putin launched a full-scale war  on ukraine claiming that he needed to  eliminate nazism in ukraine and that he  was defending the people of the donbus  regions of ukraine the international  community recognized that donbass like  crimea is part of ukraine so the  question is is this an illegal war and  what does it even mean to be an illegal  war under international law well i'm  sorry to tell you it probably doesn't  mean all that much so the phrase  international law is a bit of a misnomer  because international law isn't really  binding in the way that american laws  generally are in america the primary  sources of law are the us constitution  laws enacted by congress rules  promulgated by federal agencies state  constitutions laws enacted by state  legislatures rules promulgated by state  agencies and city or county charters and  local laws and as you can see there's  generally a hierarchy to these laws  statutes and rules can't contradict a  higher source of law for example the us  constitution is generally at the top of  that list and a state law or state  constitution that contradicts the  federal constitution where state law is  supreme is considered void but in  contrast there isn't really an  international hierarchy of law sovereign  nations may agree on general principles  of conduct but there isn't a body of  international law that is superior to  their own national laws so the first  thing you need to know about  international law is that everything is  dependent on the individual states and  as you can imagine individual sovereign  nations are incredibly reluctant to give  up any of their autonomy and to subject  themselves to the potential whims of  other countries the united states is no  exception to this  so what do we mean when we talk about  international law well the most  important sources of international law  are customary international law derived  from practice of the states treaties  between the states and general  principles of law recognized by  civilized nations now the oldest source  of what we call international law tends  to be customary international law and  this doesn't really have a written  source like a criminal code instead it's  more like english common law since it's  made up of unwritten rules that are  derived from custom and practice a rule  of customary law has two elements first  there has to be widespread and  consistent state practice the practice  must be by the government but must also  be recognized by the state's courts and  parliaments and second there has to be  what's called a pineal juris which is  the state's belief that it has a legal  obligation what's an example of  customary international law well  diplomatic immunity for example  diplomatic immunity is a principle that  gives certain foreign government  officials the right to save passage  through other countries guaranteeing the  diplomat cannot be sued or arrested for  violating the host country's law this  principle of diplomatic immunity is one  of the oldest elements of foreign  relations observed in ancient greece and  rome  sometimes now prior to world war ii  countries recognize diplomatic immunity  based simply on precedent but after  world war ii diplomatic community was  embedded in a number of international  conventions and today all countries  agree that diplomats have diplomatic  immunity although they can be expelled  from their host countries for violating  the law then we can talk about treaties  treaties are a second source of  international law an international  treaty is a written agreement between  international law subjects reflecting  their consent to the creation alteration  or termination  of their rights and obligations treaties  may also be called agreements  conventions or protocols now a treaty is  not the same thing as a law a treaty is  a source of an obligation under the law  and treaties are only binding on states  which agree to become parties to them so  there is no international tribunal or  government that can force any sovereign  nation to become a party to a treaty  against that nation's will and so if  there's no government or court that can  compel states to join or comply with the  treaty  why are treaties binding at all well  generally because of a customary  principle of international law called  pacta cervada which is latin for  agreements must be kept in i greatly  apologize for all of my mispronounced  latin throughout this entire video as it  turns out it's really hard to speak a  dead language now all states recognize  that they are bound by the agreements  that they make and therefore they are  obligated to honor their treaties but  sometimes a treaty will memorialize a  principle of customary law in essence  codifying a general understanding  between nations for example the 1969  vienna convention on the law of treaties  codified the basic customary principles  governing international treaties the  vienna convention is known as the treaty  on treaty and in the us for example a  treaty only becomes binding if congress  agrees that it should become binding and  effectively the law of the united states  so again it's about the consent of the  governed and if a country wants to back  out of a treaty there's effectively  nothing that any other country can do  but then that takes us to general  principles of civilized nations these  are general principles of fairness and  justice which are applied universally in  legal systems around the world and  although that sounds pretty sweeping in  reality these principles are pretty much  gap fillers when there's a dispute and  there is no provision in an  international treaty or statute or any  recognized customary principle of  international law the general principles  of law can be used to fill the gaps an  example of that would be the admission  of indirect evidence  in 1949 an international court of  justice decision addressed whether  albania had civil liability for the  mining of the corfu channel and  subsequent damage to two british naval  vessels the court ruled that the uk  could establish the knowledge and  responsibility of albania for laying  mines in the water through indirect  evidence since indirect evidence is  admitted in basically all systems of law  then of course there are other sort of  miscellaneous sources of international  law that may help resolve international  disputes you can look to court decisions  writings by international lawyers and  actions taken by the united nations and  its many organs but you can imagine that  if the prior sources of international  law aren't binding these certainly are  not going to have that much of a  presidential effect now i'm sure many  people are watching this video and  screaming at the screen what about the  united nations well the united nations  was established after world war ii to  provide a formal way for nations of the  world to communicate and resolve  disputes the charter of the united  nations is an international treaty that  commits member states to settle their  disputes by peaceful means many people  have the mistaken impression though that  the un general assembly is the  government of the whole world however  the united nations general assembly has  no power to legislate for the  international community its resolutions  are not legally binding the general  assembly cannot enact international laws  and then force member states to submit  to its authority u.n resolutions do  influence international relations but  they're not the equivalent of laws and  statutes so that's the united nations  general assembly but what about the un  security council and in fact the u.n  security council is the only  international body that has the power to  make binding decisions on all nations  the council has 15 members the five  permanent members are china france  russia the united kingdom and the united  states and it also has 10 non-permanent  members whose terms run for two years  each member has one vote and in 2022 the  temporary members of the security  council are the united arab emirates  albania brazil gabon ghana india ireland  kenya mexico and norway the five  permanent members all have veto power  over all u.n security council matters  and under the charter of the united  nations all member states are obligated  to comply with council decisions  therefore decisions by the u.n security  council are considered legally binding  quote under chapter 7 the council can  take measures to enforce its decisions  and ensure that mandates are fulfilled  the security council can impose economic  sanctions or order an arms embargo and  on rare occasions the council has  authorized member states to use all  necessary means including collective  military action to see that its  decisions are carried out all member  states are obligated under the u.n  charter to carry out the security  council's decision now although the  world is remarkably united about  russia's war against ukraine it will  still be difficult if not impossible for  the security council to take direct  action against russia most security  council decisions require a majority of  the nine members plus consent of all  five permanent member states russia a  permanent member vetoed a council  resolution condemning its attack on  ukraine china and india and the united  arab emirates abstained from that vote  but you can imagine that as long as  russia is a member of the un security  council no resolution is going to pass  though i will say there is an  interesting legal movement gaining some  traction which says that russia is in  fact not a member of the un security  council effectively the argument goes  like this the original member of the un  security council was the soviet union  and the soviet union collapsed though it  was succeeded by the russian federation  russia just assumed that it took over  the spot that was originally created for  the ussr so some argue that without  formalizing this relationship with  russia russia does not hold the seat  that the former soviet union held on the  security council this seems a little bit  far-fetched but we'll see if it gains  traction in the future but let's take a  moment to talk about the use of force  under international law after the  devastation of two world wars the united  nations was born the u.n charter  established a prohibition on the use of  force to resolve disputes among states  article 2 section 4 of the charter bans  the threat or use of force one against  the territorial integrity of a state to  against the political independence of a  state and three in any other manner  inconsistent with the purposes of the un  now that said there are two exceptions  to the prohibition on the use of force  the security council can use force on  behalf of the u.n to maintain peace and  security a member state can also use  self-defense to repel an armed attack  chapter 7 of the u.n charter establishes  that force may be used to keep the peace  if the security council determines that  there is the existence of a threat to  the peace a breach of the peace or an  act of aggression and efforts to address  the matter using measures short of force  have failed or would be futile now in  the early days of the u.n the charter  suggested that all member states would  make their military resources  collectively available to the security  council to use to stop wars but this is  effectively never materialized and as a  result when the council needs a  peacekeeping force willing states  volunteer their personnel  putin tried to take advantage of this  exception by declaring that his forces  were a peacekeeping force and former  president trump thought this was a great  idea and suggested that the u.s should  invade mexico with fake peacekeeping  forces as well of course this is not  what peacekeeping means under the u.n  charter as one source put it the  security council takes the lead in  determining the existence of a threat to  the peace or an act of aggression it  calls upon the parties to a dispute to  settle it by peaceful means and  recommends methods of adjustment or  terms of settlement under chapter 7 of  the un charter the security council can  take enforcement measures to maintain or  restore international peace and security  such measures range from economic  sanctions to international military  action the council also establishes u.n  peacekeeping operations and special  political missions now additionally  article 51 of the u.n charter provides  for self-defense  nothing in the present charter shall  impair the inherent right of individual  or collective self-defense if an armed  attack occurs against a member of the  united nations until the security  council has taken measures necessary to  maintain international peace and  security a sovereign nation's right of  self-defense predates international  organizations and it's one of the  fundamental principles of state  sovereignty and as part of this right  nations can rescue their own citizens  who are trapped or being held in another  country but a nation can't claim it's  merely protecting its own citizens if  its real aim is to overthrow a foreign  government now russian president putin  said that people in the donbass  republics sought russia's help and that  qualifies as self-defense but article 51  applies to states not to individuals so  as of now russia is the only state that  has recognized the two donbass republics  as their own nations though this claim  appears to be pretext for invasion and  although putin claims that the  ukrainians are committing genocide in  the donbus this also appears to be a  pretext as well and russia also claims  that ukraine is the aggressor here but  even after the invasion of crimea  ukraine did not invade or really even  threaten russia  so is the war in ukraine illegal under  international law well yes but the  principle that war is illegal is only as  strong as the international community's  willingness to intervene in the conflict  and the first modern attempt to make war  illegal was in 1928 when the u.s and  france created the kellogg-briand pact  where dozens of countries joined the  treaty which called on nations to settle  their disputes peacefully  this treaty was only as good as its  members commitment to it and of course  the pact meant nothing when world war ii  erupted a decade later the world tried  again in 1949 with the geneva  conventions the geneva conventions  govern how civilians prisoners of war  and soldiers are treated you've probably  heard that the geneva conventions  prohibit nations from torturing people  or using torture to extract information  and effectively all nations have joined  the geneva conventions but these  treaties only govern how people are  treated during war they don't say  anything about the legality of the war  itself and even though the security  council actions are said to be binding  they're only binding if the council  actually chooses to enforce them for  example the iran-iraq war in the 1980s  clearly violated the laws of war but the  security council took no steps to  intervene and then finally perhaps most  importantly there is the international  criminal court now the icc is an  independent judicial institution that  was established in 2002 it is empowered  to investigate and prosecute war crimes  crimes against humanity genocide and the  crime of aggression 123 countries are  icc members which means that these  countries have given the icc the  authority to investigate and prosecute  crimes committed by their nations or by  anyone on their territory the icc  doesn't have the power over countries  that aren't a party to the treaty  however and the u.s for example did not  join the icc president clinton signed  the rome statute the treaty authorizing  the icc but the senate wouldn't ratify  it and president george w bush then  unsigned the treaty informing the u.n  that america did not recognize any  obligations under the icc nevertheless  u.s citizens can still be prosecuted for  committing more crimes if it happens in  a territory that is an icc member  afghanistan for example is a party to  the icc and can request that the icc  investigate americans for committing war  crimes but at the same time the icc  cannot override the laws of any  sovereign nation it only exercises its  jurisdiction when a country is either  unwilling or genuinely unable to  investigate and prosecute war crimes and  of course countries are free to  prosecute war crimes in their own courts  but that being said neither russia nor  ukraine have signed or adopted the rome  treaty and are not members of the icc so  russia cannot be prosecuted for the  crime of aggression however in 2014  after russia annexed crimea ukraine  submitted a declaration giving the icc  jurisdiction for crimes committed any  time on his property after february  2014. so ukraine can ask that icc  prosecutors open a full investigation  into russia's actions  now i know when we cover international  law it often feels like there's nothing  that we can do but that's not true and  in fact if you'd like to do some good  for this world especially regarding  ukraine i'd recommend tab for cause  which is helpfully sponsoring this video  now tab for cause lets you raise money  for charity just by opening tabs and  browsing the internet which we were  probably gonna do anyway and in fact tab  for a c