s do  influence international relations but  they're not the equivalent of laws and  statutes so that's the united nations  general assembly but what about the un  security council and in fact the u.n  security council is the only  international body that has the power to  make binding decisions on all nations  the council has 15 members the five  permanent members are china france  russia the united kingdom and the united  states and it also has 10 non-permanent  members whose terms run for two years  each member has one vote and in 2022 the  temporary members of the security  council are the united arab emirates  albania brazil gabon ghana india ireland  kenya mexico and norway the five  permanent members all have veto power  over all u.n security council matters  and under the charter of the united  nations all member states are obligated  to comply with council decisions  therefore decisions by the u.n security  council are considered legally binding  quote under chapter 7 the council can  take measures to enforce its decisions  and ensure that mandates are fulfilled  the security council can impose economic  sanctions or order an arms embargo and  on rare occasions the council has  authorized member states to use all  necessary means including collective  military action to see that its  decisions are carried out all member  states are obligated under the u.n  charter to carry out the security  council's decision now although the  world is remarkably united about  russia's war against ukraine it will  still be difficult if not impossible for  the security council to take direct  action against russia most security  council decisions require a majority of  the nine members plus consent of all  five permanent member states russia a  permanent member vetoed a council  resolution condemning its attack on  ukraine china and india and the united  arab emirates abstained from that vote  but you can imagine that as long as  russia is a member of the un security  council no resolution is going to pass  though i will say there is an  interesting legal movement gaining some  traction which says that russia is in  fact not a member of the un security  council effectively the argument goes  like this the original member of the un  security council was the soviet union  and the soviet union collapsed though it  was succeeded by the russian federation  russia just assumed that it took over  the spot that was originally created for  the ussr so some argue that without  formalizing this relationship with  russia russia does not hold the seat  that the former soviet union held on the  security council this seems a little bit  far-fetched but we'll see if it gains  traction in the future but let's take a  moment to talk about the use of force  under international law after the  devastation of two world wars the united  nations was born the u.n charter  established a prohibition on the use of  force to resolve disputes among states  article 2 section 4 of the charter bans  the threat or use of force one against  the territorial integrity of a state to  against the political independence of a  state and three in any other manner  inconsistent with the purposes of the un  now that said there are two exceptions  to the prohibition on the use of force  the security council can use force on  behalf of the u.n to maintain peace and  security a member state can also use  self-defense to repel an armed attack  chapter 7 of the u.n charter establishes  that force may be used to keep the peace  if the security council determines that  there is the existence of a threat to  the peace a breach of the peace or an  act of aggression and efforts to address  the matter using measures short of force  have failed or would be futile now in  the early days of the u.n the charter  suggested that all member states would  make their military resources  collectively available to the security  council to use to stop wars but this is  effectively never materialized and as a  result when the council needs a  peacekeeping force willing states  volunteer their personnel  putin tried to take advantage of this  exception by declaring that his forces  were a peacekeeping force and former  president trump thought this was a great  idea and suggested that the u.s should  invade mexico with fake peacekeeping  forces as well of course this is not  what peacekeeping means under the u.n  charter as one source put it the  security council takes the lead in  determining the existence of a threat to  the peace or an act of aggression it  calls upon the parties to a dispute to  settle it by peaceful means and  recommends methods of adjustment or  terms of settlement under chapter 7 of  the un charter the security council can  take enforcement measures to maintain or  restore international peace and security  such measures range from economic  sanctions to international military  action the council also establishes u.n  peacekeeping operations and special  political missions now additionally  article 51 of the u.n charter provides  for self-defense  nothing in the present charter shall  impair the inherent right of individual  or collective self-defense if an armed  attack occurs against a member of the  united nations until the security  council has taken measures necessary to  maintain international peace and  security a sovereign nation's right of  self-defense predates international  organizations and it's one of the  fundamental principles of state  sovereignty and as part of this right  nations can rescue their own citizens  who are trapped or being held in another  country but a nation can't claim it's  merely protecting its own citizens if  its real aim is to overthrow a foreign  government now russian president putin  said that people in the donbass  republics sought russia's help and that  qualifies as self-defense but article 51  applies to states not to individuals so  as of now russia is the only state that  has recognized the two donbass republics  as their own nations though this claim  appears to be pretext for invasion and  although putin claims that the  ukrainians are committing genocide in  the donbus this also appears to be a  pretext as well and russia also claims  that ukraine is the aggressor here but  even after the invasion of crimea  ukraine did not invade or really even  threaten russia  so is the war in ukraine illegal under  international law well yes but the  principle that war is illegal is only as  strong as the international community's  willingness to intervene in the conflict  and the first modern attempt to make war  illegal was in 1928 when the u.s and  france created the kellogg-briand pact  where dozens of countries joined the  treaty which called on nations to settle  their disputes peacefully  this treaty was only as good as its  members commitment to it and of course  the pact meant nothing when world war ii  erupted a decade later the world tried  again in 1949 with the geneva  conventions the geneva conventions  govern how civilians prisoners of war  and soldiers are treated you've probably  heard that the geneva conventions  prohibit nations from torturing people  or using torture to extract information  and effectively all nations have joined  the geneva conventions but these  treaties only govern how people are  treated during war they don't say  anything about the legality of the war  itself and even though the security  council actions are said to be binding  they're only binding if the council  actually chooses to enforce them for  example the iran-iraq war in the 1980s  clearly violated the laws of war but the  security council took no steps to  intervene and then finally perhaps most  importantly there is the international  criminal court now the icc is an  independent judicial institution that  was established in 2002 it is empowered  to investigate and prosecute war crimes  crimes against humanity genocide and the  crime of aggression 123 countries are  icc members which means that these  countries have given the icc the  authority to investigate and prosecute  crimes committed by their nations or by  anyone on their territory the icc  doesn't have the power over countries  that aren't a party to the treaty  however and the u.s for example did not  join the icc president clinton signed  the rome statute the treaty authorizing  the icc but the senate wouldn't ratify  it and president george w bush then  unsigned the treaty informing the u.n  that america did not recognize any  obligations under the icc nevertheless  u.s citizens can still be prosecuted for  committing more crimes if it happens in  a territory that is an icc member  afghanistan for example is a party to  the icc and can request that the icc  investigate americans for committing war  crimes but at the same time the icc  cannot override the laws of any  sovereign nation it only exercises its  jurisdiction when a country is either  unwilling or genuinely unable to  investigate and prosecute war crimes and  of course countries are free to  prosecute war crimes in their own courts  but that being said neither russia nor  ukraine have signed or adopted the rome  treaty and are not members of the icc so  russia cannot be prosecuted for the  crime of aggression however in 2014  after russia annexed crimea ukraine  submitted a declaration giving the icc  jurisdiction for crimes committed any  time on his property after february  2014. so ukraine can ask that icc  prosecutors open a full investigation  into russia's actions  now i know when we cover international  law it often feels like there's nothing  that we can do but that's not true and  in fact if you'd like to do some good  for this world especially regarding  ukraine i'd recommend tab for cause  which is helpfully sponsoring this video  now tab for cause lets you raise money  for charity just by opening tabs and  browsing the internet which we were  probably gonna do anyway and in fact tab  for a cause responded to your request  and they have an entire ukraine specific  page geared towards providing essential  services and supplies to ukrainian  families and in fact save the children  has been providing ongoing humanitarian  aid in ukraine since 2014 and will  continue to support families even if  fingers crossed the conflict ends soon  now tab for a cause is a google chrome  extension that allows you to pick the  charity of your choice and then every  time you open up a new tab instead of  displaying a blank page it displays a  beautiful new customized tab with a  couple of tiny ads now the money that  you generate through those open pages is  then donated to your charity tab for a  cause has partnered with save the  children which is mobilizing now for the  ukrainian people and tab for a cause  finances are completely transparent you  can see for yourself how much money  they've raised every quarter and where  that money has gone and believe it or  not the legal eagles the people like you  who are watching my videos have already  raised over 35 000 alone for charity  just by opening tabs and i'm really  thankful for that so all you have to do  is click on the link that's on screen  right now or in the description and  install the chrome extension that's it  every page you open will create revenue  that goes to charity and it's completely  free  tap for caused users like you have  raised millions of dollars and it's the  easiest possible way to donate to  charity so just click on the link below  and download the extension and do some  good by doing what you were going to do  anyway so click on that link or i'll see  you in court
 

